{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790441899,"nonce":"hCh12aAcxhFLmrmFLVNMnJ49xeo=","title":"JIT RangeLoop miscompiles descending ranges","message":"RangeLoop rewrites `for (i in from...to)` into `i = from; while (i \u003c to) { ...; i = i + 1 }` without checking the direction. A Wren range with from \u003e to counts down, so the JIT runs the wrong number of iterations. The interpreter is right.\n\n    var c = 0\n    for (i in 100000...0) c = c + 1\n    System.print(c)            // interpreter 100000, tiered 43138 (varies with OSR timing)\n    var n = 0\n    var d = 0\n    for (i in 300000...n) d = d + 1\n    System.print(d)            // interpreter 300000, tiered 0\n\n`0...n` with n negative, `(i + 1)...size` with i \u003e= size, and `100000...0.5` are all affected.\n\nCorrect fixes tried, all costing the benchmarks (medians of 7, arm64, vs HEAD 4230925):\n- Clone the loop and pick the copy by `from \u003c= to` at the preheader: method_call 2.1x, nbody 5.1x, mandelbrot 6.9x. OSR is keyed to the original header, which became the slow generic copy.\n- Original stays the counted loop; its exit asks `from \u003e to` and a descending range continues in a cloned generic loop: nested loops 1.28x slower, because the generic copy's values flow back into the outer loop's carried values and TypeSpecialize can no longer prove them Num.\n- Same, with the copy counting down instead of generic: nested loops at parity, but method_call 1.32x, nbody 1.57x (1.38x at 5x the iterations, so steady state, not only compile time), mandelbrot 1.66x.\n- Exit to the interpreter (a GuardNumAt that cannot pass, resuming at the loop entry) instead of a copy: no code growth, but broke a nested descending case (\"Iterator must be a number\") and needs loop entries in every tier.\n- Copy only in the optimized tier: the baseline and optimized loops then have different header layouts, retier declines, and M.nested runs 8x slower in baseline.\n- Asking the range object for `from` at the exit keeps the Range allocated (DCE used to remove it) and adds a call per exit: nbody 2.7x.\n\nConstraints learned: every tier must shape a range loop the same way (retier/OSR between them); a value an OSR entry needs must be an interpreter register or a constant; values from a generic copy poison the types of enclosing loops.\n\nSuggested direction: prove ascending statically where possible (constant bounds through moves; `(i + k)...size` with k \u003c= 1 inside a loop counting i up to the same size; counts of lists) and pay for a runtime check only where it cannot be proven.\n\nAOT handles it with the counting-down copy (compile time and nesting matter less there).","files":null}]}